include "mlir/Pass/PassBase.td"

def QCToQIR : Pass<"qc-to-qir", "mlir::ModuleOp"> {
  let summary = "Lower the QC dialect to QIR in the LLVM dialect";

  let description = [{
    Lower supported QC operations to QIR runtime calls and LLVM dialect
    operations that comply with the selected QIR profile. Attach QIR attributes
    and module flags, then validate the emitted requirements against a matching
    attached target payload.

    Requirements:

    - Input is a valid module in the QC dialect.
    - The entry function must be marked with `mqt.entry_point`, have no arguments,
      and have a single return. Bind program parameters before conversion.
    - Measurements and CBit allocations must be in the entry function.
    - Explicit static qubit IDs cannot be mixed with qubit allocations.

    Behavior:

    - Outputs retain return order and repetitions. Every record has a unique label.
    - The entry function returns an `i64` zero execution status after output recording.
    - Each QC quantum operation is replaced by a call to the corresponding QIR
      function in the LLVM dialect.
    - The pass adds an initialization block, which sets up the execution
      environment, and an epilogue block, which records outputs and returns from
      the entry function. Blocks are connected by unconditional branches.
    - Non-quantum dialects are lowered via MLIR's built-in conversions.
    - QIR has no qubit layout metadata, so `mqt.layout` is dropped.

    Base Profile (`profile=base`):

    - Returns may contain only direct measurements and direct CBit allocations.
      Other scalar program outputs are not supported. A returned scalar
      measurement can share register storage only when constant indices prove
      that other stores cannot overwrite it.
    - Stores to returned CBit registers must write measurement results at indices
      defined by constant operations. Constant expressions must be folded before
      conversion; constant operations may move before the measurement.
      The store and measurement must share a block. Intervening effects must be
      limited to quantum resources or stores to distinct constant indices of the
      same register.
    - The input entry function must consist of a single block with straight-line
      control flow.
    - A measured qubit must not be used by another quantum instruction,
      including another measurement. Gates on independent qubits may follow
      measurements in the input. Reset operations are not supported.
    - Qubit-register loads require constant, in-bounds indices into statically
      sized allocations. Register aliases must be resolved before conversion.
    - Classical storage and computations must be eliminated before conversion.
      The compiler preparation pipeline promotes eligible stack arrays to SSA
      values and unrolls counted loops whose bounds and steps fold to constants,
      within a budget of one billion cloned body operations. Residual control
      flow, memory operations, runtime computations, and nonconstant call
      arguments are rejected.
    - After validating qubit usage in instruction order, the pass moves terminal
      measurements into a separate block between the gates and the epilogue.
      The emitted instructions are checked against the Base Profile subset.

    Adaptive Profile (`profile=adaptive`):

    - Returns may contain direct measurements, direct CBit allocations, and
      Boolean scalar values. Other scalar program outputs are not supported.
      Computed Boolean scalars use Boolean output records without register storage.
    - Returned registers use Result arrays when measurement stores can fuse safely
      and preserve returned measurement snapshots. Other stores use Boolean storage,
      including runtime-indexed accesses. Boolean output loads precede all output recording.
    - Counted-loop constraints are checked before lowering, including loops
      generated by classical copies. Programs containing `scf.while` require
      unconstrained emitted loop capabilities because QIR classifies loops by
      data dependence. Target metadata is preserved.
    - Switch constraints are checked before lowering. Switches exceeding those
      limits lower to conditional branches when the target permits that expansion.
    - Operations from the scf dialect are lowered into the cf dialect before they
      are lowered to the LLVM dialect. Blocks between the initialization and
      epilogue blocks keep the control flow of the entry function.
    - Quantum releases retain their original control-flow positions.
    - Local qubit-reference buffers support loads, stores, and shape casts without
      allocating qubits.
    - Measurements use static result IDs, including fixed-width returned Result
      arrays. Non-output measurements share one reusable scratch ID.
    - Measurement results may be used as classical values to drive conditional branches.
    - Constant classical tensors and element reads are lowered to constant LLVM
      arrays and loads, including reads with runtime indices. Other classical
      tensor operations must be lowered before this pass.
    - Statically shaped classical stack memrefs lower to native LLVM arrays, loads,
      and stores. Classical heap allocation is rejected. These operations require
      the Adaptive array capability and the stored element types.
    - Classical array indices must be in bounds. No runtime checks are inserted.
  }];

  let options = [Option<"profile", "profile", "::mlir::QIRProfile",
                        /*default=*/"::mlir::QIRProfile::Adaptive",
                        "QIR profile of the emitted program", [{
    ::llvm::cl::values(
        clEnumValN(::mlir::QIRProfile::Base, "base", "QIR Base Profile"),
        clEnumValN(::mlir::QIRProfile::Adaptive, "adaptive",
                   "QIR Adaptive Profile"))
  }]>];

  let dependentDialects = ["mlir::mqt::MQTDialect", "mlir::LLVM::LLVMDialect",
                           "mlir::cf::ControlFlowDialect",
                           "mlir::memref::MemRefDialect",
                           "mlir::scf::SCFDialect", "mlir::qc::QCDialect",
                           "mlir::arith::ArithDialect"];
}
